# looks-clean — line endings are decided by the repository, not by the machine.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN USUALLY. The golden layer compares a recorded
# snapshot with a fresh one field by field, fingerprints included, and a
# fingerprint is a hash of text. A checkout that rewrites LF to CRLF changes
# byte offsets inside the fixtures, which moves line numbers, which moves every
# recorded expectation — and the failure would read as "the tool changed" when
# what changed was the machine that cloned it.
#
# text=auto  — git decides what is text and normalises it to LF in the repo
# eol=lf     — and writes LF on checkout, on every platform
* text=auto eol=lf

# The recordings are data the golden layer compares byte for byte. They are
# already LF in the repository and must stay that way on every checkout.
*.json text eol=lf
